# One step taken from a bisect that is already paused at c5 (bad=c9, good=c2).
# c5 is still good and c6 is the first bad commit, so the answers below are
# fixed and the halving arithmetic is checked at a point where it is no longer
# the trivial first step.

# Marking the checked-out midpoint decides the remaining range in one move:
# good must narrow to the c6..c7 half, bad must narrow to the c3..c5 half.
bisect good
bisect bad
# `skip` cannot decide and must pick a different commit to test instead.
bisect skip
# Marking a commit other than the checked-out one is allowed and must be taken
# into account rather than ignored.
bisect good main~5
bisect bad main~2
bisect bad HEAD
bisect good HEAD
# Marking a commit outside the current range is a contradiction git reports.
bisect good main
bisect bad v1
# A revision that does not resolve must be refused before any state is written.
bisect good nosuchref
# The transcript must list the start and both endpoint markings, in order, and
# is the input `bisect replay` expects.
bisect log
bisect replay bisect-transcript.txt
bisect replay nosuchfile.txt
# The terms in force are good/bad here, so `terms` must say so and the old/new
# aliases must be rejected as the wrong vocabulary for this session.
bisect terms
bisect terms --term-good
bisect terms --term-bad
bisect terms --term-old
bisect terms --term-new
bisect old
bisect new
# `run` drives the session to completion by itself; the probe exits non-zero
# exactly on the commits carrying the bug, so it must land on c6 and print the
# full "is the first bad commit" report.
bisect run ./probe.sh
# A probe that always fails, and one that cannot be executed at all, are the
# two failure modes `run` has to distinguish.
bisect run false
bisect run ./nosuchprobe.sh
# `start` again mid-session restarts rather than compounding the state.
bisect start
bisect start main~2 main~5
# `reset` must return to the branch that was checked out before the bisect.
bisect reset
bisect reset feature
bisect reset nosuchref
